#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216} :activity/id #uuid "c10a40bf-815b-59d3-8b54-688b2275327e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048736 :topic/id #uuid "b9fc7121-4ed8-5254-ad37-ccaadac27c0e" :topic/math-academy-id 2028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130459 :tutorial/content "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1dda94b-0ea5-5ff2-a3d5-a406dfc9b95d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aae6c762-bad6-59ad-bbac-4dd3947ca34e" :step/math-academy-id 43340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128850 :question/id #uuid "915a7c2d-3797-5dfa-8d68-85e2546a23d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11778"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431765}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "a371a864-c197-5aa0-8d21-3cead8839e82" :step/math-academy-id 43341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128851 :question/id #uuid "153b828d-545a-58fb-bc81-0d7311726a87" :question/math-academy-id "e-11779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431764}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "2f9433b3-3716-5cab-88dc-0c8106c423cf" :step/math-academy-id 43342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :question/id #uuid "72ccaeab-ad76-50c7-8b51-7736c5270521" :question/math-academy-id "e-12493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431761}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"} :step/id #uuid "942eea0b-0401-5ec3-980b-a17bb6c3cdaa" :step/math-academy-id 44997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128865 :question/id #uuid "131948d9-d533-5316-bcd3-b7859244892e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431766}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "e5790220-0fbc-5caf-b3ee-9039c4289a67" :step/math-academy-id 45003 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130480 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that the moment of inertia about an axis $l$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D [r(x,y)]^2\\,\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of $D$ about the line through the center of mass $\\left(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}\\right)$ and perpendicular to the lamina is given by\n\n$$\nI_M = \\iint\\limits_D \\left((x-\\bar{x})^2+(y-\\bar{y})^2\\right) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a08f490-ccca-5a93-adb8-dbf845e4d4cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7849 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Moment of Inertia About the Line Through the Center of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "f23fbe74-8ade-5fcf-9513-af6482f642b2" :step/math-academy-id 45317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250}}] :activity/title "The Parallel Axis Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129370}]}
